        "Attributes": [
          {
            "Id": 1230358,
            "attributeType": "LongString",
            "attributeName": "AIDescription",
            "attributeRelationType": "None",
            "Value": "The Sega Dreamcast, released in Japan in 1998 and globally in 1999, marked Sega's final foray into home console hardware. As the first sixth-generation console, it innovated with features like a built-in modem for online play and the use of GD-ROM discs to reduce production costs. Sharing architecture with Sega's NAOMI arcade system board, the Dreamcast delivered authentic arcade experiences at home. While hampered by competition from the PlayStation 2 and limited third-party support, the Dreamcast left a lasting legacy. \n\nPopular titles like *Sonic Adventure*, *Crazy Taxi*, *Shenmue*, *Jet Set Radio*, and *Phantasy Star Online* showcased its capabilities. Despite selling 9.13 million units worldwide, Sega discontinued the Dreamcast in 2001, transitioning to a third-party developer role. Today, it's celebrated for its pioneering online features and innovative game design."
          },
